EXT. THE MARGARET ANN - NORTH SEA - DAY

Grey water. Grey sky. Gulls.

A restored 1890s sailing smack, brown sails patched, bobbing hard. The crew wear wool jerseys and oilskins of the period, a living-history charter for tourists.

SAL, 60, skipper, white beard, stands at the tiller. Weather in every wrinkle.

JEN, 25, pulls a net hand over hand. MO, 19, coils rope badly.

Between them, near the mast, a WOMAN sits on an upturned crate. Mid-30s. A city coat, no oilskin. Dry shoes. She watches the horizon like she's counting something.

Spray.

JEN
(to Mo)
Not like that. Over, under.

MO
That's what I did.

JEN
It's not.

Mo tries again. Worse.

SAL
Leave him, Jen. Sea'll teach him.

JEN
Sea'll drown him.

SAL
Same lesson.

The Woman almost smiles.

Wind. Canvas SNAPS.

SAL (CONT'D)
(to the Woman)
You'll want to move, miss. Boom's coming round.

THE WOMAN
I'm fine.

SAL
You're not.

She doesn't move.

Silence.

A gull. Then another.

The boom swings. Fast.

Jen dives. Catches the Woman by the collar. They roll across the deck.

The boom sails over, an inch above.

Quiet. Everyone breathing.

JEN
(shaking)
Are you out of your mind?

THE WOMAN
(flat)
Probably.

Sal lashes the boom. Hard hands, hard face.

SAL
What's your name, miss?

THE WOMAN
Ro.

SAL
Rosalind?

RO
Just Ro.

MO
(staring)
You weren't scared.

RO
I was.

Another engine, from behind the fog.

Out of the mist comes a harbour patrol launch. At the rail, BIG DAVE, 50s, enormous in a yellow slicker, peaked cap, a clipboard in a fist like a shovel.

BIG DAVE
(through a megaphone)
Margaret Ann! Permit check!

SAL
(muttering)
Of course.

Big Dave looks across the water. Finds Ro. His expression softens.

BIG DAVE
(not on the megaphone)
Rosalind. Your mother's been calling the harbour all morning.

Ro closes her eyes.

JEN
(to Ro, quietly)
Your mother?

RO
I wanted to see where she grew up.

Mo goes back to his rope. Over, under. Right this time.

Rain begins.
